{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>When a ruler sins.</b> The word אשר has the force of אם ('if'), and its sense is: if a ruler sins. So too (Deuteronomy 11:27): ״את הברכה אשר תשמעו״ — the sense of which is: if you obey." "<b>Or.</b> It is possible to say that it is a term of certainty, and therefore it was not written 'if a ruler sins,' as he said 'if the anointed priest sins' and 'and if the whole congregation of Israel errs,' for the High Priest is cautious regarding sin because the eyes of all Israel are dependent upon him — both for matters of their bodily needs and sustenance, and for matters of atonement for their souls — and likewise the Sanhedrin are also cautious regarding sin because the holy spirit rests upon them in addition. Therefore in their case Scripture expressed their sinning in language of doubt. But in the case of the ruler, Scripture expressed the sin in language of certainty, because the ruler's heart is very haughty within him, and the trait of pride — which is the cause of sin — is found with him because of the greatness of his dominion. Therefore the Torah admonished the king and rebuked him for this trait, as it is written (Deuteronomy 17:20): ״לבלתי רום לבבו מאחיו״. And because of this it is written (Exodus 35:27): ״והנשיאים הביאו את אבני השהם״ — what is the significance of the fact that they volunteered stones more than other things? Rather, because the stones were upon Aaron's heart as a remembrance for them before HaShem, and the princes' hearts were haughty within them, therefore they brought stones so that atonement might be made for them." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> אשר נשיא יחטא — אשר in the sense of אשרי ('happy'). Rabban Yohanan ben Zakkai said: Happy is the generation whose ruler brings a sin-offering for his inadvertence. If the ruler brings a sin-offering for his inadvertence, need one say what he brings for an intentional sin? If the ruler brings a sin-offering, need one say what an ordinary person requires? 'A ruler' — one might think the prince of a tribe, such as Nahshon; therefore Scripture says: ״ועשה אחת מכל מצות ה׳ אלהיו״, and below it says (Deuteronomy 17:19): ״למען ילמד ליראה את ה׳ אלהיו״. Just as the ruler mentioned below has none above him but HaShem his Elohim, so too the ruler mentioned here has none above him but HaShem his Elohim. And so you find in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 12:12): ״והנשיא אשר בתוכם אל כתף ישא״, and its meaning is the king, and the intention is Zedekiah king of Judah, because the king is the prince among the people. And this is what is written (Exodus 22:27): ״אלהים לא תקלל ונשיא בעמך לא תאור״ — He admonished first concerning the blessing of the Supreme King and afterward concerning the king who reigns on earth. And the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, interpreted that when the people sin, the king is held accountable for their sin, as it is written: ״חטאת הקהל הוא״, and adjacent to it: ״אשר נשיא יחטא״. From here they said: anyone who has the power to protest and does not protest is held accountable for everything."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:01.667591223Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}